    "prompt": "Function `_pipepager` in src/click/_termui_impl.py. The statements below were written about this exact version of the function.\n\nFunction source (docstring removed):\n```python\ndef _pipepager(generator: cabc.Iterable[str], cmd: str, color: bool | None) -> None:\n    import subprocess\n\n    env = dict(os.environ)\n\n    # If we're piping to less we might support colors under the\n    # condition that\n    cmd_detail = cmd.rsplit(\"/\", 1)[-1].split()\n    if color is None and cmd_detail[0] == \"less\":\n        less_flags = f\"{os.environ.get('LESS', '')}{' '.join(cmd_detail[1:])}\"\n        if not less_flags:\n            env[\"LESS\"] = \"-R\"\n            color = True\n        elif \"r\" in less_flags or \"R\" in less_flags:\n            color = True\n\n    c = subprocess.Popen(cmd, shell=True, stdin=subprocess.PIPE, env=env)\n    stdin = t.cast(t.BinaryIO, c.stdin)\n    encoding = get_best_encoding(stdin)\n    try:\n        for text in generator:\n            if not color:\n                text = strip_ansi(text)\n\n            stdin.write(text.encode(encoding, \"replace\"))\n    except (OSError, KeyboardInterrupt):\n        pass\n    else:\n        stdin.close()\n\n    # Less doesn't respect ^C, but catches it for its own UI purposes (aborting\n    # search or other commands inside less).\n    #\n    # That means when the user hits ^C, the parent process (click) terminates,\n    # but less is still alive, paging the output and messing up the terminal.\n    #\n    # If the user wants to make the pager exit on ^C, they should set\n    # `LESS='-K'`. It's not our decision to make.\n    while True:\n        try:\n            c.wait()\n        except KeyboardInterrupt:\n            pass\n        else:\n            break\n```\n\nFacts extracted from this version by a parser (tree-sitter):\nsignature: _pipepager(generator, cmd, color) -> None\ncalls: ' '.join, c.wait, cmd.rsplit, cmd.rsplit(\"/\", 1)[-1].split, dict, get_best_encoding, os.environ.get, stdin.close, stdin.write, strip_ansi, subprocess.Popen, t.cast, text.encode\nreturn statements: (none)\nraise statements: (none)\ncontrol flow: if=3 loops=2 try=2 with=0 returns=0 yields=0 awaits=0\n\n\nStatements:\nS1. The `_pipepager` function is a utility within the Click library.\nS2. The `_pipepager` function pipes the output of a generator to an external command.\nS3. The `_pipepager` function takes three parameters: `generator`, `cmd`, and `color`.\nS4. `generator` is an iterable of strings to be displayed.\nS5. `cmd` is the command to which the output will be piped.\nS6. `color` is a boolean or `None` indicating whether to preserve ANSI color codes in the output.\nS7. The `_pipepager` function returns nothing (`None`).\nS8. The function relies on the `subprocess` module to execute the command and handle the input/output streams.\nS9. The function handles `OSError` and `KeyboardInterrupt` exceptions gracefully.\nS10. The function includes a loop to wait for the subprocess to complete.\nS11. The function potentially modifies the environment and alters the terminal state.\nS12. The function's purpose is to handle the display of potentially large amounts of text in a user-friendly manner.\n\nFor each statement decide:\n- supported: the shown code makes the statement true (a statement about purpose/intent counts as supported unless the code contradicts it).\n- contradicted: the shown code clearly makes the statement false, e.g. a named parameter, call, attribute, return value, exception or condition it describes no longer exists or now behaves differently.\n- unresolved: the statement concerns code that is not shown (callers, helper internals, external systems) and the shown code neither confirms nor refutes it.\nBe strict about \"contradicted\": require concrete evidence in the shown code or the diff; renaming a local variable or reformatting does not contradict a statement about behaviour.\n\nAnswer with exactly one line per statement and nothing else, in thi